<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corded Ware culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2900–2300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/30th-century-bce">30th century BCE</a> · Also known as Single Grave culture, Battle Axe culture, Boat Axe culture, Schnurkeramik-Kultur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Corded Ware culture was a broad archaeological horizon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between c. 2900 and 2300 BCE, from the Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, reaching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the east. Friedrich Klopfleisch named it in 1883 after the cord-like impressions on its pottery; it is also called the Single Grave culture, after its burials under tumuli in a crouched position, and the Battle Axe culture, after the stone axe placed in men's graves. Genetic studies suggest that it arose from the westward migration of Yamnaya-related people from the steppe, and it is considered a likely vector for the spread of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corded-ware-culture.jpg" alt="Corded Ware culture" width="330" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maptist · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corded_Ware_Culture.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In its early phase it was the western neighbour of the <b>Corded Ware culture</b> of central Europe, and its spread became one of the central questions in the twentieth-century debate between migration and the diffusion of ideas.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Populations of the <b>Corded Ware</b>, Bell Beaker, Sintashta and Andronovo cultures drew large parts of their ancestry from the Yamnaya or a closely related population, with up to 75% Yamnaya-like ancestry in Corded Ware skeletons.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
